===
title: "No commercially available autonomous seeder can plant a quarter-acre plot for le"
subTitle: claimground v0.0.4 export, verdict SUPPORTED
===

/* Export only. Argdown's '+' means support; claimground's
   need edge means NECESSARY CONDITION. '-' = attack, exact
   match. Source receipts ride in {braces} metadata. */

[c1]: No commercially available autonomous seeder can plant a quarter-acre plot for less than ten times the cost of hired labor in its first season
  + [c2]: The cheapest garden-scale autonomous seeder sells for $7,995 #leaf {sources: ["s1 @ product pricing section near top of page [LIVE]"]}
  + [c3]: That seeder's maximum footprint is 3 by 6 meters, 18 square meters #leaf {sources: ["s1 @ Nominal machine dimensions section [LIVE]"]}
  + [c4]: Field-scale autonomous seeders cost about $60,000 and are designed for fields of 20 to 30 hectares #leaf {sources: ["s5 @ Scaleable with more robots feature section [LIVE]", "s6 @ [PRICE] ALERT section and main product header [LIVE]"]}
  + [c5]: Hired crop labor costs $17.15 per hour at the US median #leaf {sources: ["s3 @ Wages & Employment Trends section, 2025 BLS wage data [LIVE]"]}
  + [c6]: A walk-behind push seeder plants a full 30-inch bed in one or two walking passes #leaf {sources: ["s4 @ Description section under main product heading [LIVE]"]}
  + [c7]: Even by the vendor's own ROI analysis, measured against retail produce prices, FarmBot's direct-cost payback takes years, not one season #linchpin #leaf {sources: ["s7 @ Analyzing Direct Costs section [LIVE]"]}
  - [a1]: A commercially available seeder exists that plants a quarter acre cheaply enough to bring the gap under tenfold #hole
  - [a2]: FarmBot's maker publishes an ROI analysis showing the robot pays for itself #leaf {sources: ["s7 @ Scaling to the MAX section [LIVE]"]}
